clearcote: WebGPU adapter.features follow the claimed GPU model.

The persona spoofed the GPU identity strings while navigator.gpu still exposed the HOST
adapter's feature set -- an NVIDIA feature list next to an Intel UHD 770 string, readable in one
page. Real Windows captures of the two models the persona serves (audit corpus, decontaminated)
report one modal set each: UHD 770 17 features in 19 of 21 captures (no shader-f16, no
subgroups), RTX 3060 19 features in 5 of 7. The persona carries that list for the model its
FINAL renderer string names; a capture's own webgpu.features list is authoritative; an unknown
model leaves the list empty and the real adapter's set is served. gpu_adapter.cc serves the
INTERSECTION of the list with the real adapter's features -- subtractive only, because
requestDevice(requiredFeatures) is validated by Dawn against the real adapter and an exception
would be a worse tell than a missing feature. The validator requires the model's list and
rejects shader-f16/subgroups on a UHD 770 claim.

